  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given SignalR mode X (per task 001's decision) and a connected test client subscribed as user U, when a producer writes an outbox row and then calls the delivery service's ping entrypoint for U, then the connected client receives an at-most-once, signal-only message within the seam test's timeout.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given SignalR is disabled or unreachable (null-object registered), when a producer calls the ping entrypoint after its own outbox write, then the call returns without throwing and without blocking the producer's own success path.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given an authenticated user calls POST /api/notifications/negotiate, when the request succeeds, then the returned connection info is scoped to that user's own oid only — no request parameter can target a different user's connection (verified: a request with a spoofed target-user field, if any is accepted at all, is ignored server-side).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given an unauthenticated request to POST /api/notifications/negotiate, when it is sent, then the endpoint returns 401 and no connection info is returned.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the delivery service and negotiate endpoint are registered, when the BFF host starts with SignalR OFF (null-object path), then ASP.NET Core minimal-API endpoint metadata generation succeeds at startup (no unconditional-endpoint-vs-conditional-service abort per ADR-032).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BFF publish measured ≤60 MB compressed (report absolute size + diff vs ~49.63 MB incl-PDB baseline, state the PDB convention used); 0 new HIGH-severity CVE from `dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive`; Placement Justification stated per .claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A tests/integration/seam/Notifications/** test exists that exercises both the outbox-before-ping ordering and the null-object degrade path as an executable seam test (not a unit-level mock substitute per ADR-038's banned-test-class list).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the ping entrypoint's payload is inspected, when compared against the envelope contract from task 013, then it carries NO message body, NO privileged content, and NO pre-authorized action token — only routing/signal information (NFR-02/03).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Mode decision file: notes/spikes/fr-01-signalr-footprint.md. Envelope shape belongs to task 013 — do not design envelope fields here. Group(...) targeting is a primitive this task exposes; task 023 owns the actual fan-out/targeting decision logic on top of it.</notes>

